  <div class="callout">Variable names are for humans. Addresses are for machines. Pointers are where the two meet.</div>

  <p>This sounds philosophical until you realise what falls out of it. Once you see variables as addresses, whole categories of bugs stop being mysterious. Why does passing an array to a function not copy it? Because the name of the array decays into a pointer, and you're only ever sending the address. Why does modifying something inside a function sometimes affect the caller and sometimes not? Because one version was passed by address and the other was passed by value. You stop memorising rules and start deducing them.</p>

